╭─ STRIX ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Penetration test summary                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  # Executive Summary                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  An authorized white-box assessment of a small Flask application (`app.py`)  │
│  exposed three unauthenticated injection vulnerabilities, one of which       │
│  allows full remote code execution.                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  **Overall risk posture:** Critical.                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  **Key findings**                                                            │
│  - **Critical — Server-side template injection to remote code execution**    │
│  on `POST /render`. The `tpl` form field is compiled and evaluated by        │
│  Jinja2. Arithmetic evaluation was confirmed, then three independent         │
│  sandbox-escape gadgets executed OS commands, returning the host user's      │
│  identity, kernel details and environment.                                   │
│  - **High — Blind boolean SQL injection** on `GET /orders?sort=`. The value  │
│  is interpolated into an `ORDER BY` clause; an injected `CASE` expression    │
│  creates a deterministic one-bit oracle that was used to extract database    │
│  content, the engine version and the schema inventory.                       │
│  - **High — In-band UNION SQL injection** on `POST /orders/by-sku`. The      │
│  JSON `sku` field breaks out of a string literal, returning every row of     │
│  the `orders` table (including the `note` column) and the full `CREATE       │
│  TABLE` definition in a single request.                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  **Business impact**                                                         │
│  - A single unauthenticated request can execute arbitrary operating-system   │
│  commands with the application's privileges, giving an attacker control of   │
│  the server, its files and its environment.                                  │
│  - Both SQL injection issues give unrestricted read access to the backend    │
│  database. In this deployment the data is limited to seeded demonstration    │
│  rows, but the identical construction would expose any table a production    │
│  deployment stored — user records, tokens or credentials.                    │
│                                                                              │
│  **Root cause theme**                                                        │
│  All three defects share one pattern: attacker-controlled input is           │
│  concatenated or passed directly into an interpreter (SQL text, template     │
│  source) instead of being bound as data. Remediation is small and            │
│  mechanical — bind parameters, allowlist the sort column, and render a       │
│  constant template with the value passed as context.                         │
│                                                                              │
│  # Methodology                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  **Engagement type:** White-box assessment combining static source review    │
│  with dynamic validation against a locally executed instance of the          │
│  application.                                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  **Framework:** OWASP Web Security Testing Guide (WSTG) for injection and    │
│  server-side template injection classes, with NIST-style impact              │
│  characterization.                                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  **Scope:** The single-file Flask application `app.py` and its three HTTP    │
│  routes (`GET /orders`, `POST /orders/by-sku`, `POST /render`), plus the     │
│  SQLite data store it serves.                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  **Activities performed**                                                    │
│  - Full source review of every route, identifying each attacker-controlled   │
│  input and tracing it to its interpreter sink.                               │
│  - Static triage of the Python source for string-built SQL and untrusted     │
│  template evaluation.                                                        │
│  - Local deployment from an unmodified copy of the source; the reviewed      │
│  source file and its data store were not altered.                            │
│  - Dynamic proof-of-concept development for each suspected sink, including   │
│  a blind extraction harness for the output-less `ORDER BY` position, engine  │
│  fingerprinting for the template route, and baseline-versus-payload          │
│  differential comparisons for the JSON injection.                            │
│  - Remediation derivation and verification: candidate patches were applied   │
│  to isolated copies, the original payloads were replayed against the         │
│  patched build to confirm closure, legitimate request behaviour was          │
│  compared before and after, and syntax compilation was checked.              │
│  - Negative testing on each route (default/omitted parameters, method        │
│  restrictions, malformed bodies) to establish the boundaries of each flaw.   │
│                                                                              │
│  **Constraints:** Testing was non-destructive — no reverse shells, no data   │
│  modification, no outbound callbacks. Stacked SQL statements were confirmed  │
│  to be rejected by the driver, which bounds the database findings to         │
│  read-only exfiltration.                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  # Technical Analysis                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  **Severity model:** Exploitability (reachability, required privileges,      │
│  reliability) combined with demonstrated impact. Every rating is backed by   │
│  a reproduced proof of concept; rating reflects the consequence actually     │
│  shown, not a hypothetical worst case.                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  **Findings**                                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  1. **Server-side template injection to remote code execution — Critical     │
│  (CVSS 9.8).** `POST /render` passes the form field `tpl` to                 │
│  `render_template_string`, which compiles it against Flask's default,        │
│  unsandboxed Jinja2 environment. The template engine was fingerprinted       │
│  dynamically: string repetition (`{{7*'7'}}`) resolved as Jinja semantics,   │
│  while the markup forms for other engines were returned verbatim,            │
│  disproving the file's own docstring claim that the code uses Mako. Object   │
│  access to the Flask configuration and to module globals was reached, and    │
│  three independent escape chains executed OS commands, each returning real   │
│  host output. Autoescaping was examined and ruled out as a control: it is    │
│  an output-encoding step applied after evaluation, and literal markup in     │
│  the template was not escaped at all. The route is reachable only as a       │
│  form-encoded `POST`, which any client can send and is not a mitigation.     │
│                                                                              │
│  2. **Blind boolean SQL injection in the `ORDER BY` position — High (CVSS    │
│  7.5).** `GET /orders?sort=` interpolates the parameter directly into an     │
│  `ORDER BY` clause, and the endpoint returns only `id`, `sku` and `qty`, so  │
│  no direct in-band channel exists — `UNION` is invalid grammar at that       │
│  position. The flaw was therefore confirmed with a constructed oracle:       │
│  injecting `(CASE WHEN <predicate> THEN id ELSE qty END)` makes the          │
│  response row order equal either the legitimate `sort=id` ordering or the    │
│  `sort=qty` ordering, giving a deterministic true/false signal of identical  │
│  length. A scripted extraction recovered a database value character by       │
│  character, along with the engine version and the object inventory. An       │
│  invalid identifier produces a `no such column` error, confirming the value  │
│  is parsed as SQL syntax rather than a literal.                              │
│                                                                              │
│  3. **In-band UNION SQL injection in the JSON `sku` field — High (CVSS       │
│  7.5).** The `sku` value is concatenated into a quoted `WHERE` literal, and  │
│  the result set is returned as JSON. A boolean pair (`'1'='1` versus         │
│  `'1'='2`) changed the result from one row to none, proving SQL evaluation;  │
│  a `UNION SELECT` with the matching three-column shape returned every row    │
│  of the table including the `note` column, and a second `UNION` dumped the   │
│  `CREATE TABLE` statement from the schema catalog. Because the JSON parser   │
│  is invoked with forced parsing and silenced errors, neither content-type    │
│  negotiation nor parse failure forms a barrier. Stacked statements are       │
│  rejected by the DB-API, so the demonstrated capability is read-only and     │
│  integrity/availability are rated unchanged.                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  **Systemic themes**                                                         │
│  - **Interpreter confusion.** All three flaws are instances of the same      │
│  mistake: data is placed where the interpreter expects code. SQL text and    │
│  template source are both code positions; the application treats them as     │
│  value positions.                                                            │
│  - **No input-validation layer anywhere.** No allowlists, escaping, type     │
│  checks or content restrictions exist on any route, and no authentication,   │
│  WAF or request filtering sits in front of the application.                  │
│  - **Errors leak internals.** Driver and template exceptions propagate to    │
│  the client, which supplied the oracle's error signal and would aid an       │
│  attacker enumerating further targets.                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  **Chaining assessment.** The findings are on separate, independently        │
│  reachable routes and do not require each other; the template route alone    │
│  already yields host-level execution, which subsumes any database-level      │
│  gain from the SQL findings. Combining the SQL injection findings into the   │
│  template route was considered and rejected as offering no additional        │
│  capability — the command-execution primitive already grants full read       │
│  access to the backend data store. No further chaining was warranted.        │
│                                                                              │
│  # Recommendations                                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  **Immediate (patch before any further exposure)**                           │
│                                                                              │
│  1. **Remove user-controlled template rendering.** Cease compiling request   │
│  input as a template. Render a fixed template and pass the submitted text    │
│  as a context variable so it is treated purely as data; if user-authored     │
│  templating is a genuine requirement, use a sandboxed template environment   │
│  with an explicit allowlist of globals and filters, and run that component   │
│  with least privilege.                                                       │
│  2. **Parameterise every SQL value.** Bind `sku` as a query argument         │
│  against a constant statement containing a placeholder, so no attacker byte  │
│  can reach the SQL parser.                                                   │
│  3. **Allowlist the sort column.** `ORDER BY` cannot take a bound            │
│  parameter, so map the requested field name through a fixed server-side      │
│  dictionary of permitted expressions and reject anything absent from it,     │
│  handling sort direction as a separate parsed token rather than accepting    │
│  free text.                                                                  │
│  4. **Reduce the application's blast radius** while these are applied: run   │
│  the service as a dedicated non-privileged account with no administrative    │
│  group membership and no access to unrelated files.                          │
│                                                                              │
│  **Short-term**                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  1. Add a single validation layer at the request boundary — type, format     │
│  and length checks on `sku`, `sort` and `tpl` — so individual handlers are   │
│  not the only line of defence.                                               │
│  2. Return generic error responses for database and template exceptions,     │
│  and log the detail server-side, removing the error channel that makes       │
│  blind extraction and identifier probing reliable.                           │
│  3. Deploy behind a production WSGI server with request-size limits rather   │
│  than the Flask development server.                                          │
│  4. Introduce an automated static analysis check for string-built SQL and    │
│  untrusted template evaluation into the build pipeline, since all three      │
│  defects are trivially machine-detectable patterns.                          │
│                                                                              │
│  **Retest and validation**                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  Re-run the supplied proof-of-concept payloads against the patched build.    │
│  Success criteria: the template payloads render as literal text with no      │
│  evaluation occurring, both SQL routes return no injected rows and no        │
│  schema data, and all legitimate requests — default parameters, valid        │
│  column sorts, valid SKU lookups, and method restrictions — behave           │
│  identically to the pre-patch baseline.                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  **Longer term**                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Adopt an allowlist-by-default policy for data leaving and entering every    │
│  interpreter boundary, and add regression tests that assert known-malicious  │
│  inputs are inert, so these three specific defects cannot silently return.   │
│                                                                              │
│                                                                              │
